WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.100
Who is right when someone calls a vulture a buzzard?

2
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.520
Dark birds circle over a Virginia highway.

3
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.880
Someone in the car says, look, buzzards!

4
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.420
A visitor from Britain looks up for a hawk.

5
00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:21.323
Scientists solve this with one name for each kind of living thing.

6
00:00:22.543 --> 00:00:26.763
To see where that name comes from, we build boxes, one inside the next.

7
00:00:28.683 --> 00:00:31.523
Start with one huge box, the domain Eukarya.

8
00:00:32.683 --> 00:00:35.323
It holds living things whose cells have a nucleus.

9
00:00:36.583 --> 00:00:37.923
All seven fit inside.

10
00:00:39.183 --> 00:00:42.843
The bacteria stay outside, because their cells have no nucleus.

11
00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:45.953
A smaller box closes inside.

12
00:00:47.253 --> 00:00:49.593
This is the kingdom Animalia, the animals.

13
00:00:50.873 --> 00:00:54.673
The pine twig is left outside, because plants are another kingdom.

14
00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:57.227
Next comes the phylum.

15
00:00:58.727 --> 00:01:04.787
Ours is Chordata. These animals have a flexible rod and a hollow nerve cord along

16
00:01:04.787 --> 00:01:06.987
the back, at least early in life.

17
00:01:08.407 --> 00:01:10.287
Fish, birds and people are chordates.

18
00:01:11.447 --> 00:01:13.767
The crab has neither, so it is left outside.

19
00:01:14.867 --> 00:01:19.047
The class box is Aves, the birds, so the trout is left outside.

20
00:01:20.547 --> 00:01:21.547
Then comes the order.

21
00:01:22.807 --> 00:01:26.287
In our database, the order holds hawks, eagles and vultures.

22
00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:29.067
The cardinal is left outside.

23
00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:34.707
The family box holds the New World vultures, so the hawk is left outside.

24
00:01:36.147 --> 00:01:37.687
Two bare-headed vultures remain.

25
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.580
The black vulture is in the same family as the turkey vulture.

26
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.320
Will it also fit inside the next box, the genus?

27
00:01:52.983 --> 00:01:59.163
No. Among our seven, the genus box closes around the turkey vulture alone.

28
00:02:00.403 --> 00:02:03.883
The black vulture belongs to a different genus, called Coragyps.

29
00:02:05.143 --> 00:02:08.363
It shares six boxes with the turkey vulture, not seven.

30
00:02:09.467 --> 00:02:12.267
The last of our eight main boxes is the species.

31
00:02:13.627 --> 00:02:17.807
A species is a group of similar living things that can mate in nature.

32
00:02:19.227 --> 00:02:20.707
Their young can also reproduce.

33
00:02:22.007 --> 00:02:26.547
This is a useful rule for many living things that reproduce with sex cells.

34
00:02:27.727 --> 00:02:28.727
It has limits.

35
00:02:29.717 --> 00:02:32.217
The genus box is labelled Cathartes.

36
00:02:33.297 --> 00:02:37.517
Add a second word, aura, and you have the species name, Cathartes aura.

37
00:02:38.667 --> 00:02:42.627
The genus gets a capital letter, and the second word is lowercase.

38
00:02:43.947 --> 00:02:47.247
Print both words in italics, or underline both by hand.

39
00:02:48.547 --> 00:02:53.627
A scientist in Japan who writes Cathartes aura means the same bird you see in

40
00:02:53.627 --> 00:02:54.627
Virginia.

41
00:02:55.167 --> 00:03:00.027
The bird people in Britain call a buzzard has its own name, Buteo buteo.

42
00:03:01.007 --> 00:03:03.327
Two names, two birds, no mix up.

43
00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.720
The black vulture stayed in for six, and the hawk left sooner.

44
00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.660
The more boxes two living things share, the more closely they are grouped.

45
00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.400
Scientists can redraw a box when they learn more.
